# To run several resolver identities from this one code dir, put each one's
# config in its own file (e.g. .env.gemini) and select it at runtime with
#   RESOLVER_ENV_FILE=.env.gemini python resolve_tickets.py
# (a relative path is taken next to the resolver; default is .env). .env.* files
# are gitignored except this .env.example.

# --- Stingray connection -------------------------------------------------
# Base URL the resolver uses for the API. Two cases:
#   * Same host as the backend (local dev): point at the backend DIRECTLY with
#     no /api prefix, e.g. http://localhost:8000
#   * Remote (resolver on a dev station, Stingray on a server): use the PUBLIC
#     site URL WITH /api, e.g. https://tickets.example.com/api — the nginx
#     frontend proxies /api to the backend over HTTPS. Don't expose the backend
#     port publicly; keep it internal and go through /api.
STINGRAY_URL=http://localhost:8000
# API key for this resolver's dedicated bot member user (mint via the API once).
STINGRAY_API_KEY=sk_replace_me
# This resolver's bot user id (from the user-create response). Each resolver
# identity gets its OWN bot user so multiple resolvers (e.g. a Claude box and a
# Gemini/opencode box, or 2-3 stations) sweep disjoint ticket queues and never double-
# process. Legacy name CLAUDE_BOT_USER_ID still works as a fallback.
RESOLVER_BOT_USER_ID=2

# --- Agent ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Which coding agent this resolver drives. Built in: "claude" (Claude Code) and
# "opencode" (the opencode CLI, model-agnostic — e.g. Gemini). Register more in
# agents.py (see CodexRunner). One agent per resolver identity.
RESOLVER_AGENT=claude

# --- Repo allowlist ------------------------------------------------------
# Hard boundary: the resolver will only ever operate on repos *inside* this
# directory. A ticket's `repo:<name>` tag resolves to PROJECTS_ROOT/<name>.
PROJECTS_ROOT=/home/penguin/projects
# Optional explicit name=path overrides (comma-separated). Paths must still
# resolve inside PROJECTS_ROOT.
#   REPO_MAP=ticketing=/home/penguin/projects/ticketing,site=/home/penguin/projects/my-site
REPO_MAP=
# Repo used when a ticket carries no `repo:<name>` tag. A bare name is looked
# up under PROJECTS_ROOT; leave blank to require an explicit repo: tag.
DEFAULT_REPO=

# --- Agent CLI -----------------------------------------------------------
# These are agent-neutral. The legacy CLAUDE_* names (CLAUDE_BIN, CLAUDE_MODEL,
# CLAUDE_IMPLEMENT_TOOLS, CLAUDE_TIMEOUT, CLAUDE_IMPLEMENT_TIMEOUT) still work as
# fallbacks, so an existing Claude resolver needs no change.
#
# CLI binary to invoke: "claude" (default) or "opencode".
AGENT_BIN=claude
# Optional model override. Claude: passed to `claude --model`. opencode: use the
# provider/model form, e.g. google/gemini-2.5-flash. Blank = CLI default.
AGENT_MODEL=
# Optional per-phase model overrides. Each is blank by default and falls back to
# AGENT_MODEL, so leaving them empty changes nothing. The plan and review phases are
# read-only exploration (a cheaper/smaller model is usually fine); implement does the
# edits and verification (use your strongest model). Same value format as AGENT_MODEL.
AGENT_PLAN_MODEL=
AGENT_IMPLEMENT_MODEL=
AGENT_REVIEW_MODEL=
# Difficulty-routed implement tiers. The plan self-assesses easy|medium|hard; the
# implement phase then routes by it. An `easy` ticket implements on AGENT_IMPLEMENT_MODEL_EASY
# (use a cheap model), a `hard` one on AGENT_IMPLEMENT_MODEL_HARD — but only when
# escalation is OFF; with ESCALATE_TO_USER_ID set, hard tickets are handed to the Claude
# bot instead (see below). Blank = no swap (falls back to AGENT_IMPLEMENT_MODEL); routing
# is opt-in. medium always uses AGENT_IMPLEMENT_MODEL.
AGENT_IMPLEMENT_MODEL_EASY=
AGENT_IMPLEMENT_MODEL_HARD=
# opencode-only: models to escalate through after the primary fails a run (a
# swallowed 503/overload, a hang/timeout, or a run that explored but produced no
# answer). The resolver tries the primary, then each of these in order, one attempt
# each, before handing the ticket back (or off to another resolver). Prefer the
# plural, comma-separated AGENT_FALLBACK_MODELS to chain several free models so one
# unreliable model can fall through to alternatives. AGENT_FALLBACK_MODEL (singular)
# is still honored and appended for back-compat. Blank = no escalation.
# e.g. AGENT_FALLBACK_MODELS=google/gemini-2.0-flash,google/gemini-2.5-pro
AGENT_FALLBACK_MODELS=
AGENT_FALLBACK_MODEL=
# Tools the agent may use during the *implement* phase (space-separated allowlist).
# Broad `Bash` lets the agent run the project's tests/build; isolation comes from
# the per-ticket git worktree + the PROJECTS_ROOT allowlist, not from narrowing
# Bash (a narrow allow like `Bash(pytest:*)` is defeated by `cd X && pytest`).
# Claude-only (opencode uses its own permission model). The plan phase is always
# read-only regardless of this list.
AGENT_IMPLEMENT_TOOLS=Edit Write Read Glob Grep Bash
# Per-ticket timeout, seconds. Legacy general budget; if AGENT_PLAN_REVIEW_TIMEOUT
# is unset it also governs the read-only plan/review phases.
AGENT_TIMEOUT=1800
# Timeout for the *implement* phase, seconds. Implement does strictly more than
# plan (edit files + run the test suite), so it gets a larger budget.
AGENT_IMPLEMENT_TIMEOUT=2400
# Timeout for the read-only plan/review phases, seconds. These are exploration only
# and should finish in a couple of minutes, so keep this short: a stalled/hung model
# is then abandoned quickly and the fallback chain (AGENT_FALLBACK_MODELS) moves on
# instead of holding the resolver lock for the full implement budget. Defaults to
# AGENT_TIMEOUT when unset, else 600.
AGENT_PLAN_REVIEW_TIMEOUT=600

# --- opencode agent (only when RESOLVER_AGENT=opencode) ------------------
# Named opencode agents for each phase. "plan" is permission-restricted (no
# edit/bash) so the read-only plan phase cannot modify the repo; "build" is
# unrestricted for the implement phase. Override only if you define custom agents.
OPENCODE_PLAN_AGENT=plan
OPENCODE_BUILD_AGENT=build
# Authenticate opencode against each provider with `opencode auth login` (keys are
# stored in ~/.local/share/opencode/auth.json). For reliability, point AGENT_MODEL /
# AGENT_FALLBACK_MODELS at *different providers* (Mistral, OpenRouter, Gemini, …) so
# their independent free quotas don't 429 together like models in one Google project.
# CAVEAT: opencode sends a ~32k-token system prompt every call, so the AGENT path
# needs a high per-minute token budget — low-TPM free tiers (e.g. Groq free, 6–12k
# TPM) 429 with ContextOverflowError before they start. Use those for REVIEW_API_*
# (single-shot reviews, small prompt) below, not as AGENT_MODEL.

# --- Difficulty routing (free resolver -> Claude) ------------------------
# Lets a free/secondary resolver hand the hard or important tickets to the Claude
# bot instead of attempting them. Set ESCALATE_TO_USER_ID to the Claude bot's user
# id (0/unset = disabled; the Claude resolver leaves it unset so it never escalates
# to itself). A ticket escalates when its priority is in ESCALATE_PRIORITIES, OR it
# carries the `dangerous` tag, OR it is manually tagged `claude`, OR — at implement
# time — its approved plan self-assessed the work as `hard` (see AGENT_IMPLEMENT_MODEL_HARD).
ESCALATE_TO_USER_ID=
ESCALATE_PRIORITIES=high,critical

# --- /consolidate directive ----------------------------------------------
# A `/consolidate [PR#...]` line branches off the repo's default branch, merges
# the given (or all open) PRs onto it, opens a "consolidation" PR, and files a
# code-review ticket on the result, assigned to this user id. Default 4
# (claude-lite-ubvm).
CONSOLIDATE_REVIEW_USER_ID=4

# --- Single-shot review backend (optional) -------------------------------
# When all three are set, code_review tickets are reviewed via ONE OpenAI-compatible
# /chat/completions call (the code_blocks are in the prompt — no tools needed) rather
# than the agent loop: more reliable and provider-agnostic (Groq/Mistral/OpenRouter).
# Blank = review through the configured agent.
REVIEW_API_URL=
REVIEW_API_KEY=
REVIEW_API_MODEL=

# --- Plan-critique gate (optional) ---------------------------------------
# When all three are set, a cheap chat-completion model vets each freshly produced
# plan BEFORE the human (and the expensive implement run) sees it. On a REVISE verdict
# the planner is re-invoked with the critique's notes, up to CRITIQUE_MAX_REVISIONS
# times (default 1); the verdict is appended to the plan comment. Same OpenAI-compatible
# shape as REVIEW_API_* — point it at a cheap/fast model (Groq/Mistral/OpenRouter).
# Fail-open: a quota'd/flaky/unparseable critique never blocks a produced plan. Blank =
# gate off (plans go straight to the human — the legacy behavior).
CRITIQUE_API_URL=
CRITIQUE_API_KEY=
CRITIQUE_API_MODEL=
CRITIQUE_MAX_REVISIONS=1

# --- Daily digest (optional) ---------------------------------------------
# `digest.py` files one report ticket summarizing a slice of the backlog (see
# digests.toml / digests.example.toml and README -> "Daily digest"). It is a
# separate entrypoint on its own schedule, NOT part of the sweep.
#
# DIGEST_ADMIN_KEY must be an ADMIN user's API key. The digest surveys the whole
# tracker, and the API shows non-admins only tickets they created or are assigned
# to — so running it on the resolver's own key silently produces a digest of the
# bot's queue instead of the backlog. `digest.py` refuses to start without this,
# and warns loudly if the key turns out not to be an admin's. Blank = no digest.
#
# The digest talks only to the API, so it can run anywhere with network access —
# it needs STINGRAY_URL and DIGEST_ADMIN_KEY and nothing else. A host whose only
# job is filing a digest against a remote instance does NOT need a bot id,
# PROJECTS_ROOT, an agent CLI, or any repos. See README -> "Against an instance
# hosted elsewhere".
DIGEST_ADMIN_KEY=
# The summary paragraph. Same OpenAI-compatible shape as REVIEW_API_*; each field
# falls back to its REVIEW_API_* counterpart, so a resolver that already has a
# cheap review model needs nothing here. With none of them set the digest still
# files — checklist and counts, minus the prose.
DIGEST_API_URL=
DIGEST_API_KEY=
DIGEST_API_MODEL=

# --- Verification gate (optional) ----------------------------------------
# After an implement run, the resolver runs VERIFY_COMMAND in the worktree to
# confirm the agent's changes actually pass before publishing. On failure it
# re-invokes the agent with the test output (up to VERIFY_MAX_RETRIES repair
# attempts); if it still fails, the PR/hand-back is published but prominently
# flagged "⚠️ Tests failing" so a human takes over. Blank = gate off (publish as
# soon as there's a diff — the legacy behavior).
#
# IMPORTANT: the implement worktree is a FRESH `git worktree`, so gitignored
# artifacts (.venv, node_modules) are NOT present. The command must be
# self-contained — point at an absolute interpreter, use `uv run`, or bootstrap
# its own venv. e.g.:
#   VERIFY_COMMAND=cd backend && /abs/path/.venv/bin/pytest -q --rootdir=$PWD -p no:cacheprovider
VERIFY_COMMAND=
VERIFY_TIMEOUT=900
VERIFY_MAX_RETRIES=1

# --- Sandboxing (optional) -----------------------------------------------
# When set, wraps the agent subprocess during the *implement* phase in a
# namespace jail so untrusted ticket content cannot escape the worktree even
# via arbitrary shell commands. {cwd} is substituted with the worktree path.
#
# Requires bubblewrap (bwrap) — install with: sudo apt install bubblewrap
# The profile below makes the worktree writable, the rest of the filesystem
# read-only, and hides /home and /tmp with empty tmpfs so credentials stored
# there are invisible to the agent.
#
#   SANDBOX_COMMAND=bwrap --bind {cwd} {cwd} --ro-bind / / --proc /proc --dev /dev --tmpfs /tmp --tmpfs /home --
#
# Alternative: firejail
#   SANDBOX_COMMAND=firejail --noprofile --whitelist={cwd} --
#
# Blank = no sandbox (legacy behavior — isolation relies on path anchoring
# and the post-run escape check only).
SANDBOX_COMMAND=

# --- GitHub (optional) ---------------------------------------------------
# If the target repo has a pushable 'origin' and `gh auth status` succeeds,
# the resolver opens a PR. Otherwise it falls back to a local branch (or, if
# PATCH_FALLBACK=1, posts a diff as a comment and makes no repo writes).
PATCH_FALLBACK=0
# Identity stamped on the resolver's commits. Set explicitly so commits don't
# fail on a host with no global git identity.
GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=Stingray-Resolver
GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=resolver@polarstingray.dev
# Longer timeout (seconds) for network git/gh commands (push/fetch/pr create),
# so a slow transfer isn't killed by the default command budget.
GIT_NET_TIMEOUT=300

# --- Reliability ---------------------------------------------------------
# Retry transient Stingray API failures (connection reset, timeout, HTTP
# 429/500/502/503/504) this many times with exponential backoff before failing.
STINGRAY_MAX_RETRIES=3
# Give up auto-retrying a ticket after this many failed plan/implement attempts
# and hand it back to a human (status=open). 0 disables the cap.
MAX_ATTEMPTS=3
# Process at most this many tickets per sweep so a backlog doesn't serialize for
# hours under the flock; the next cron tick picks up the rest. 0 = unlimited.
MAX_TICKETS_PER_SWEEP=0
# When an agent run fails on an API quota / rate limit (e.g. a free-tier 429)
# rather than a real error, the resolver parks the ticket — keeping it assigned
# to the bot and preserving its phase tag — instead of handing it back. The sweep
# skips the ticket for this many minutes, then auto-retries the same phase, so a
# quota outage needs no human intervention. Re-assign the ticket to retry sooner.
QUOTA_BACKOFF_MINUTES=60

# --- Resolver-to-resolver delegation (fan-out) ---------------------------
# Lets a "lead" resolver decompose a ticket into sub-tasks and hand each to
# another resolver. STRICTLY OPT-IN and gated two ways: this flag must be on,
# AND the ticket must carry the `delegate` tag (a reserved control tag, so only
# you/an admin or a resolver bot can set it). When both hold, the lead audits,
# files one `dangerous` sub-task per issue assigned to a chosen resolver, posts a
# roll-up on the parent and hands it back to you — you review the resulting PRs.
# Children are one level only: they implement+open a PR but cannot fan out again.
RESOLVER_ALLOW_DELEGATION=0
# Roster of resolvers the lead may delegate to: `id:name:desc` entries separated
# by `;`. The lead's agent reads this to route each sub-task (heavy→claude bot,
# cheap mechanical→open bot). Empty ⇒ no targets, delegation stays disabled.
#   RESOLVER_WORKERS=2:claude:heavy refactors & multi-file changes;3:open:cheap mechanical single-file fixes
RESOLVER_WORKERS=
# Hard cap on sub-tasks a single delegation run may file (guards against runaway
# fan-out / cost). Enforced in file_ticket.py via the parent:<id> child count.
RESOLVER_MAX_DELEGATIONS=10

# --- Logging / audit -----------------------------------------------------
# Log lifecycle (runs at sweep start): each finished day's loose sweep/audit/
# ticket logs are rolled into logs/archive/<date>.tar.gz, and archives older
# than LOG_RETENTION_DAYS are deleted. Empty sweeps leave no files at all.
# View per-ticket logs (loose or archived) with ./logs.py — see README.
LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=14
# Archive loose logs from days older than this. 1 = keep today loose, batch
# yesterday and older into daily tarballs. 0 disables archiving.
LOG_ARCHIVE_AFTER_DAYS=1
# This bot's own cron stdout log. When set, it's size-rotated to <path>.1 at
# sweep start (no external logrotate needed). Point each identity at ITS file
# (the Claude bot -> cron.log; a second bot -> cron-gemini.log). A relative
# path is taken next to the resolver. Unset = no rotation.
#   CRON_LOG=logs/cron.log
CRON_LOG=
# Rotate CRON_LOG once it exceeds this many bytes (default 5 MB).
CRON_LOG_MAX_BYTES=5000000
# Max bytes of command / agent output copied into the structured audit log per
# event (the full output still lands in the per-ticket log files).
AUDIT_OUTPUT_TAIL_BYTES=4096
